
<% content_for :title, "On Medications | Bipolar Sober" %>

<style>
  .medications-page {
    background: #080808;
    color: #fff;
    font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
  }

  .medications-page *,
  .medications-page *::before,
  .medications-page *::after {
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }

  /* HERO */

  .medications-hero {
    min-height: 85vh;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 80px 6%;
    background-color: #080808;
    background-image:
      linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(8, 8, 8, .65), transparent),
      url("<%= asset_path('bipolar_medications_desktop.png') %>");
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: center;
  }

  .medications-hero-content {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 1200px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .medications-eyebrow {
    max-width: 440px;
    margin: 0 0 28px;
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1.6;
    letter-spacing: .12em;
  }

  .medications-hero h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(58px, 10vw, 150px);
    font-weight: 900;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: .88;
    letter-spacing: -.065em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .medications-hero h1 span {
    display: block;
  }

  .medications-intro {
    margin: 32px 0 0;
    font-size: clamp(15px, 1.6vw, 22px);
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  /* QUOTE */

  .medications-quote {
    padding: 48px 6%;
    background: #080808;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .medications-quote blockquote {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .medications-quote blockquote p {
    margin: 0 0 12px;
    color: #eaff00;
    font-size: clamp(30px, 5vw, 64px);
    font-weight: 900;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.15;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .medications-quote cite {
    font-size: 14px;
    font-style: normal;
  }

  /* SECTIONS */

  .medications-section {
    padding: 72px 6%;
  }

  .medications-section-inner {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, .85fr) minmax(0, 1.15fr);
    gap: 60px;
    max-width: 1200px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .medications-number {
    margin: 0 0 22px;
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: .12em;
  }

  .medications-section h2 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(36px, 4.5vw, 68px);
    font-weight: 900;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: .95;
    letter-spacing: -.045em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    overflow-wrap: break-word;
  }

  .medications-copy {
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .medications-copy p {
    margin: 0 0 22px;
  }

  .medications-copy p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  /* COLORS */

  .medications-pink {
    background: #ff1493;
    color: #080808;
  }

  .medications-yellow {
    background: #eaff00;
    color: #080808;
  }

  .medications-purple {
    background: #8a00ff;
    color: #fff;
  }

  .medications-black {
    background: #080808;
    color: #fff;
  }

  .medications-white {
    background: #fff;
    color: #080808;
  }

  /* TAKEAWAYS */

  .medications-takeaway {
    padding-top: 22px;
    border-top: 3px solid currentColor;
    font-weight: 800;
  }

  /* FOOTER */

  .medications-footer {
    padding: 40px 6%;
    border-top: 1px solid #444;
    background: #080808;
  }

  .medications-footer-inner {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 24px;
    max-width: 1200px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .medications-footer a {
    color: #eaff00;
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1.5;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .medications-footer a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .medications-footer a:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid #ff1493;
    outline-offset: 6px;
  }

  /* MOBILE */

  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .medications-hero {
      min-height: 88svh;
      align-items: flex-start;
      padding: 48px 6% 60px;
      background-image:
        url("<%= asset_path('bipolar_medications_mobile.png') %>");
      background-position: center bottom;
    }

    .medications-eyebrow {
      margin-bottom: 24px;
      font-size: 10px;
    }

    .medications-hero h1 {
      font-size: clamp(48px, 12vw, 90px);
    }

    .medications-intro {
      margin-top: 24px;
      font-size: 14px;
    }

    .medications-section {
      padding: 48px 6%;
    }

    .medications-section-inner {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 28px;
    }

    .medications-section h2 {
      font-size: clamp(36px, 10vw, 58px);
    }

    .medications-copy {
      font-size: 16px;
    }

    .medications-number {
      margin-bottom: 16px;
    }
  }
</style>


<main class="medications-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="medications-hero" aria-labelledby="medications-title">
    <div class="medications-hero-content">

      <p class="medications-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / BIPOLAR / MEDICATIONS
      </p>

      <h1 id="medications-title">
        <span>ON</span>
        <span>MEDICATIONS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="medications-intro">
        FIVE AND A HALF YEARS.<br>
        A WHOLE LOT OF TRIAL AND ERROR.<br>
        AND FINALLY, SOMETHING THAT WORKS.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- CARRIE FISHER QUOTE -->
  <section class="medications-quote" aria-label="Opening quote">
    <blockquote>
      <p>"“What's in the horse pill?”</p>
      <cite>— Randle McMurphycite>
    </blockquote>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / THE CAROUSEL OF MEDICINE -->
  <section class="medications-section medications-pink">
    <div class="medications-section-inner">

      <div class="medications-section-heading">
        <p class="medications-number">
          01 / THE CAROUSEL OF MEDICINE
        </p>

        <h2>
          ROUND AND<br>
          FUCKING<br>
          ROUND.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="medications-copy">

        <p>
          Taking your medication was never the problem.
          Finding the right medication was. For five to
          five and a half years, you went through what
          felt like an endless carousel of medications,
          dosages, adjustments, and waiting.
          Try this. Increase that. Decrease something
          else. Give it a few weeks. See how you feel.
          Repeat.
        </p>

        <p>
          One medication doesn't work, so you try another.
          One dosage isn't enough, so it gets increased.
          Then the side effects show up, and you're
          adjusting everything all over again.
          Meanwhile, your brain is still doing whatever
          the hell it wants.
        </p>

        <p>
          And you're expected to be patient through
          the whole thing. As though spending years
          experimenting with your brain chemistry
          is just another ordinary inconvenience.
          It isn't.
        </p>

        <p class="medications-takeaway">
          YOU TAKE YOUR MEDS.<br>
          THAT WAS NEVER THE PROBLEM.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / THE WAITING -->
  <section class="medications-section medications-yellow">
    <div class="medications-section-inner">

      <div class="medications-section-heading">
        <p class="medications-number">
          02 / THE WAITING GAME
        </p>

        <h2>
          IS IT<br>
          WORKING<br>
          YET?
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="medications-copy">

        <p>
          You start a medication and then you wait.
          And wait. And wait some more.
          Maybe you notice something immediately.
          Maybe you spend weeks trying to figure out
          whether anything has changed at all.
        </p>

        <p>
          Then you finally get enough time on the
          medication to know whether it's helping,
          only to discover that the dosage needs
          adjusting or something else needs replacing.
          Congratulations. You get to start
          the waiting game all over again.
        </p>

        <p>
          Meanwhile, your life doesn't stop.
          You still have responsibilities, relationships,
          moods, and symptoms. You can't put bipolar
          disorder on hold while a medication
          decides whether it's going to work.
        </p>

        <p class="medications-takeaway">
          YOUR BRAIN DOESN'T GIVE A SHIT<br>
          ABOUT THE ADJUSTMENT SCHEDULE.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / SIDE EFFECTS -->
  <section class="medications-section medications-purple">
    <div class="medications-section-inner">

      <div class="medications-section-heading">
        <p class="medications-number">
          03 / THE SIDE EFFECTS
        </p>

        <h2>
          NOTHING<br>
          FITS.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="medications-copy">

        <p>
          As if finding the right medication
          weren't frustrating enough, you also
          get to deal with what it does to your body.
          Weight gain is a particularly obnoxious
          example.
        </p>

        <p>
          Your body changes. Your clothes stop fitting.
          Suddenly, getting dressed becomes another
          reminder that you have bipolar disorder.
          You're taking something to help your brain,
          and now you're dealing with a completely
          different problem because of it.
        </p>

        <p>
          And yes, it's frustrating. You're allowed
          to be annoyed that none of your clothes fit.
          You're allowed to hate that your body
          has changed because of medication.
          It's another consequence of treatment
          that people who haven't experienced
          it may never fully understand.
        </p>

        <p class="medications-takeaway">
          YOU'RE TREATING YOUR BRAIN.<br>
          YOUR BODY GETS DRAGGED ALONG.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / ANTIDEPRESSANTS -->
  <section class="medications-section medications-black">
    <div class="medications-section-inner">

      <div class="medications-section-heading">
        <p class="medications-number">
          04 / WHEN MEDICATION BACKFIRES
        </p>

        <h2>
          WELL,<br>
          THAT WENT<br>
          TO SHIT.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="medications-copy">

        <p>
          Sometimes a medication doesn't just fail
          to help. Sometimes it makes everything
          considerably worse.
        </p>

        <p>
          You know what it's like to have an
          antidepressant send your brain completely
          haywire. Something prescribed to improve
          your mental health ended up destabilizing
          it instead. Sometimes bipolar rage steps in.
        </p>

        <p>
          Antidepressants can sometimes trigger
          mania or worsen mood instability in
          people with bipolar disorder.
          You know firsthand why medication
          changes aren't always minor adjustments.
          Sometimes the consequences are enormous.
        </p>

        <p>
          It adds another layer of frustration. 
          You take the medication
          because you're trying to manage your
          illness, and the medication itself
          becomes part of the problem.
        </p>

        <p class="medications-takeaway">
          THE THING THAT WAS SUPPOSED TO HELP<br>
          MADE YOUR BRAIN GO OFF THE WALL.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / OTHER PEOPLE -->
  <section class="medications-section medications-white">
    <div class="medications-section-inner">

      <div class="medications-section-heading">
        <p class="medications-number">
          05 / THE FRUSTRATION
        </p>

        <h2>
          MUST<br>
          BE FUCKING<br>
          NICE.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="medications-copy">

        <p>
          Most people don't have to spend years
          finding a combination of psychiatric
          medications just to establish some
          stability. They don't have to think
          about dosages, side effects, mood
          destabilization, and whether the
          latest adjustment is actually working.
        </p>

        <p>
          They get to go about their lives
          without a constant reminder that
          their brain requires this much
          maintenance. Meanwhile, you've spent
          years dealing with appointments,
          adjustments, waiting periods,
          unexpected reactions, and physical
          changes you never asked for.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can accept that medication is
          important and still be pissed off
          about how much effort it took to
          get the right combination.
          Those things aren't contradictory.
        </p>

        <p class="medications-takeaway">
          YOU CAN BE GRATEFUL IT WORKS<br>
          AND STILL HATE THE PROCESS.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / FINDING THE RIGHT COMBINATION -->
  <section class="medications-section medications-pink">
    <div class="medications-section-inner">

      <div class="medications-section-heading">
        <p class="medications-number">
          06 / FINALLY, SOMETHING WORKS
        </p>

        <h2>
          FIVE AND<br>
          A HALF<br>
          YEARS LATER.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="medications-copy">

        <p>
          After roughly five and a half
          years of trying different medications,
          adjusting dosages, dealing with side
          effects, and waiting for results,
          you finally found a combination
          that works for you.
        </p>

        <p>
          That doesn't mean bipolar disorder
          disappeared. You still have symptoms
          to recognize, impulses to manage,
          and responsibilities to handle.
          Medication isn't a magical cure
          that makes every difficult part
          of the illness vanish.
        </p>

        <p>
          But you aren't constantly starting
          over with another medication,
          another dosage, and another round
          of waiting. You've reached a point
          where your treatment routine is
          established, and taking your meds
          is simply something you do.
        </p>

        <p>
          It took years to get here.
          You're allowed to appreciate
          finally having something that works
          while remembering exactly how
        exhausting it was to find it.
        </p>

        <p class="medications-takeaway">
          THE CAROUSEL FINALLY SLOWED DOWN.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 07 / THE BIGGER PICTURE -->
  <section class="medications-section medications-yellow">
    <div class="medications-section-inner">

      <div class="medications-section-heading">
        <p class="medications-number">
          07 / THE BIGGER PICTURE
        </p>

        <h2>
          PART OF<br>
          YOUR LIFE.<br>
          NOT ALL OF IT.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="medications-copy">

        <p>
          Medication is one part of living with
          bipolar disorder. You still have to
          pay attention to your moods, sleep,
          judgment, and impulsivity. You still
          have to deal with difficult episodes
          and the consequences of your decisions.
        </p>

        <p>
          Finding the right combination doesn't
          guarantee that nothing will ever go
          wrong again. And if symptoms change,
          your psychiatrist needs to know.
          You don't have to make medication
          adjustments on your own.
        </p>

        <p>
          Your treatment is important, but
          it isn't your entire identity.
          You've spent enough time thinking
          about what your brain needs.
          You also get to have a life beyond
          appointments, prescriptions,
          and medication bottles.
        </p>

        <p class="medications-takeaway">
          YOU HAVE BIPOLAR DISORDER.<br>
          YOU ALSO HAVE A WHOLE FUCKING LIFE.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- NAVIGATION -->
  <nav class="medications-footer" aria-label="Bipolar topic navigation">
    <div class="medications-footer-inner">

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO BIPOLAR",
          bipolar_path %>

      <%= link_to "NEXT: ON MOOD SWINGS →",
          bipolar_mood_swings_path %>

    </div>
  </nav>

</main>
